  "query_preview": "..\nMuch of Southeast Arizona is under partly cloudy skies. This is\nthanks to the lingering moisture in the area. There have been a\nfew cloud build-ups in the mountains, with most of the build-ups\nin the White and Chiricahua Mountains. As for thunderstorms, \nthe activity has remained to Northern Arizona and East in New \nMexico. Can't rule out a few showers/storms this afternoon and \nevening, but no where near the intensity and coverage we have \nseen over the past few days. \n\nThe downward trend in thunderstorm activity sticks around \nthrough Friday as the high pressure reconsolidates. As the\nthreat for storms decreases, temperatures begin to rise.\nTemperatures will slowly increase into late this week into the\nweekend, with the hottest days expected on Friday and Saturday.\nTemperatures will range from 105 to 112 degrees in the Tucson \nMetro, Western Deserts, and Upper Gila River Valley. Elsewhere \ntemperatures will range from 100 to 105 degrees. This increase \nin temperatures in combination with the warm low temperatures of\n80 to 85 degrees lead to hoisting up an Extreme Heat Warning \nfor Friday and Saturday when temperatures will be 5 to 10 \ndegrees above normal. Major HeatRisk is expected for the \nvalleys, so be aware of the signs for heat illness/stroke, stay \nhydrated, stay indoors with air conditioning when possible, and \nconsider moving plans indoors during the hottest time of day.\n\nThe combination of moisture increasing and a favorable upper \nair pattern will bring back more widespread chances for \nthunderstorms this weekend. The high pressure sets up in the 4\ncorners allowing for SE flow and moisture to work into the area.\nWind and dust look to be the greatest impacts this weekend, but\nisolated flash flooding can't be ruled out. More details will\ncome into focus as we get closer to the weekend.",
